## Libraries and Data

You will begin by loading the libraries you will use. The `summarytools` library is used for getting a full statistical description of your data frame, while `tidyverse` is used for all the statistical analysis, including graphs.

```{r}
#| output: false
library(summarytools)
library(tidyverse)
library(ggplot2)
```

Now, you load your data set into the variable `movies`. Remember to have your data set in the same folder as this Quarto document.

```{r}
movies <- read.csv("Team1_movies_dataset.csv")
```

## Exploring the dataset

You can explore the contents of the data frame just by typing in its name. However, since the data set contains around 200 observations, we can get a glipse of the information by displaying only the first 6 observations with the function `head()`.

```{r}
head(movies)
```

The function `dim()` will give you the number of rows and columns of the data frame. The first entry is the number of rows (observations), and the second entry is the number of columns (attributes).

```{r}
dim(movies)
```

-   *How many observations are there in your data frame? [200]{.underline}*

-   *How many attributes each observation has? [31]{.underline}*

### Extracting variables of interest

Next, you will create two variables, `imdb` and `personal`, containing the rating given by the Internet Movie Database and your personal rating for the movies in your data frame. First, we use the function `names()` to extract the name of all the attributes in the data frame.

```{r}
names(movies)
```

If the column for IMDb ratings is called `IMDB`, you can extract such column with `movies$IMDB`.

```{r}
imdb <- movies$IMDb
personal <- movies$Personal
```

## Descriptive statistics

### Minimum and maximum

Minimum and maximum can be found thanks to the `min()` and `max()` functions, or with `range()`. Note that if there is missing data (NA), you must include the option `na.rm = TRUE`.

##### IMDb ratings

```{r}
range(imdb, na.rm = TRUE)
```

-   *What is the minimum IMDb rating? [31]{.underline}*

-   *What is the maximum IMDb rating? [93]{.underline}*

##### Personal ratings

```{r}
range(personal, na.rm = TRUE)
```

-   *What is the minimum personal rating? [20]{.underline}*

-   *What is the maximum personal rating? [100]{.underline}*

### Measures of central tendency

#### Mean

The mean can be computed with the `mean()` function

```{r}
mean(imdb, na.rm = TRUE)
mean(personal, na.rm = TRUE)
```

-   *What is the average IMDb rating? [74.05]{.underline}*

-   *What is your average personal rating? [81.45732]{.underline}*

*Tips:*

-   If there is at least one missing value in your dataset, use `mean(data, na.rm = TRUE)` to compute the mean with the NA excluded. This argument can be used for many other functions, not only the mean.

#### Median

The median can be computed thanks to the `median()` function:

```{r}
median(imdb, na.rm = TRUE)
median(personal, na.rm = TRUE)
```

-   *What is the median of IMDb ratings? [75]{.underline}*

-   *What is the median of your personal ratings? [80]{.underline}*

### Measures of variation

#### Range

The range of the data can be easily computed by subtracting the minimum from the maximum

```{r}
max(imdb, na.rm = TRUE) - min(imdb, na.rm = TRUE)
max(personal, na.rm = TRUE) - min(personal, na.rm = TRUE)

```

-   *What is the range of IMDb ratings? [62]{.underline}*

-   *What is the range of your personal ratings? [80]{.underline}*

#### Interquartile range

The interquartile range (i.e., the difference between the first and third quartiles) can be computed with the `IQR()` function

```{r}
IQR(imdb, na.rm = TRUE)
IQR(personal, na.rm = TRUE)
```

-   *What is the interquartile range of IMDb ratings? [11.25]{.underline}*

-   *What is the interquartile range of your personal ratings? [12.5]{.underline}*

#### Standard deviation and variance

The standard deviation and the variance are computed with the `sd()` and `var()` functions, respectively. These functions correspond to the sample standard deviation and variance, since R assumes that the data corresponds to a sample of the population.

```{r}
var(imdb, na.rm = TRUE)
var(personal, na.rm = TRUE)
sd(imdb, na.rm = TRUE)
sd(personal, na.rm = TRUE)

```

-   *What is the variance of IMDb ratings? [85.64573]{.underline}*

-   *What is the variance of your personal ratings? [138.1147]{.underline}*

-   *What is the standard deviation of IMDb ratings? [9.254498]{.underline}*

-   *What is the standard deviation of your personal ratings? [11.75222]{.underline}*

### Descriptive statistics of several variables at a time

We can use `descr()` to display all relevant statistics of all numerical values in the dataset. Use it in your data frame.

```{r}
descr(imdb)
descr(personal)
```

Look at the row labeled with `Skewness`, and answer the following questions.

