-   *Is the variable* `personal` *skewed to the left or to the right? [left]{.underline}*

## Graphical analysis

Now that you have a numerical description of your data frame, you can proceed to visualize this information. You are asked to draw a histogram and a boxplot for both variables, `imdb` and `personal`. Use the grammar of graphics from the `ggplot2` library for this, using piped data. Remember to include a proper title for your graphs, as well as labels for the x and y axis. You are at liberty to choose any color scheme, as long as it does not get in the way of conveying the needed information.

### Histogram

A histogram gives an idea about the distribution of a quantitative variable. The idea is to break the range of values into intervals and count how many observations fall into each interval. Histograms are a bit similar to barplots, but histograms are used for quantitative variables whereas barplots are used for qualitative variables.

```{r}
# Type the code for drawing a histogram for IMDb ratings with 10 bins here
chart_imdb <- ggplot(movies, aes(x = imdb))
chart_imdb + 
  geom_histogram(aes(y=after_stat(density)),bins = 10,color = 1, fill = "white") +
  #geom_density(alpha = 0.25,lwd = 1, colour = "4",
  #             fill = "blue") +
  labs(title = "Histogram for IMDb ratings",
       x = "Ratings",
       y = "Density")
```

-   *Does the skewness of the histogram agree with the skewness parameter given in the description of the data above? [Yes]{.underline}*

```{r}
# Type the code for drawing a histogram for your personal ratings with 10 bins here
chart_personal <- ggplot(movies, aes(x = personal))
chart_personal + 
  geom_histogram(aes(y=after_stat(density)),bins = 10,color = 1, fill = "white") +
  #geom_density(alpha = 0.25,lwd = 1, colour = "4",
  #             fill = "blue") +
  labs(title = "Histogram for personal ratings",
       x = "Ratings",
       y = "Density")
```

-   *Does the skewness of the histogram agree with the skewness parameter given in the description of the data above? [Yes]{.underline}*

### Boxplots

Boxplots are really useful in descriptive statistics and are often underused (mostly because it is not well understood by the public). A boxplot graphically represents the distribution of a quantitative variable by visually displaying five common location summary (minimum, median, first/third quartiles and maximum) and any observation that was classified as a suspected outlier using the interquartile range (IQR) criterion.

The IQR criterion means that all observations above $q_{0.75}+1.5\cdot IQR$ or below $q_{0.25}-1.5\cdot IQR$ (where $q_{0.25}$ and $q_{0.75}$ correspond to first and third quartile respectively) are considered as potential outliers by R. The minimum and maximum in the boxplot are represented without these suspected outliers.

Seeing all these information on the same plot help to have a good first overview of the dispersion and the location of the data.

```{r}
# Type the code for drawing a boxplot for IMDb ratings
movies |>
  ggplot(aes(x=imdb)) +
  geom_boxplot()
```

-   *Are there any outliers for this variable?* [Yes, there are 2]{.underline}

```{r}
# Type the code for drawing a boxplot for your personal ratings 
movies |>
  ggplot(aes(x=personal)) +
  geom_boxplot()
```

-   *Are there any outliers for this variable?* [Yes, there are 4]{.underline}

